(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Background: Patients with pre-dialysis chronic kidney disease often develop thoracic) Tj
T*
(volume restriction and respiratory muscle weakness, potentially contributing to exercise) Tj
T*
(limitation and dyspnea. Whether progressive inspiratory muscle training can improve) Tj
T*
(respiratory physiology in this population remains unclear. Methods: Thirty adults with CKD) Tj
T*
(stages 3-5 on conservative treatment will be randomly assigned to two training protocols:) Tj
T*
(experimental group \(EG, n=15\) receiving progressive resistance loading up to 50% of) Tj
T*
(maximal inspiratory pressure, increased weekly, or control group \(CG, n=15\) performing) Tj
T*
(fixed 5 cmH2O loading without progression. Both groups train daily for 8 weeks. Assessment) Tj
T*
(timepoints: baseline and week 8. Primary outcome: thoracic cavity volumes \(pulmonary, rib) Tj
T*
(cage, abdominal compartments\) measured via non-invasive optoelectronic plethysmography.) Tj
T*
(Secondary outcomes: maximal inspiratory and expiratory pressures measured via standard) Tj
T*
(manometry. Safety will be monitored throughout including vital signs and adverse symptoms.) Tj
T*
(Inclusion: confirmed CKD stage 3-5, stable renal function and medications >=4 weeks,) Tj
T*
(ability to perform breathing maneuvers. Study design: randomized controlled trial with) Tj
T*
(parallel-group design.) Tj
